{
 "fps": 30,
 "lead": 0.8,
 "tail": 2.0,
 "footer": "Sources: canada.ca \u00b7 CRA TD1 (more than one employer or payer); filing TD1 forms; CRA T4127 (2026); EI premium rates and maximums (2026) \u00b7 Examples, general info, not advice",
 "vo": [
  {
   "id": "L1",
   "file": "vo/L1.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Lena works two jobs. Together, they pay her seventy-five thousand dollars a year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L2",
   "file": "vo/L2.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Both employers took tax off every single paycheque. All year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L3",
   "file": "vo/L3.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And in the spring, her tax return says she still owes about five thousand and ten dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L4",
   "file": "vo/L4.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Nobody made a mistake. Not her employers, and not the C R A."
  },
  {
   "id": "L5",
   "file": "vo/L5.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And two small forms would have fixed almost all of it."
  },
  {
   "id": "L6",
   "file": "vo/L6.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Let's take her year apart, one paycheque at a time."
  },
  {
   "id": "L7",
   "file": "vo/L7.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "By the end of this video, you'll know exactly why two jobs so often end in a tax bill, and how to set up your paycheques so it doesn't happen to you."
  },
  {
   "id": "L8",
   "file": "vo/L8.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "All numbers: twenty twenty-six, Ontario, employment income, paid every two weeks. Examples, not real people. General information, not advice."
  },
  {
   "id": "L9",
   "file": "vo/L9.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "First: how payroll sees you."
  },
  {
   "id": "L10",
   "file": "vo/L10.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Each employer only sees its own job. It has no idea the other one exists."
  },
  {
   "id": "L11",
   "file": "vo/L11.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "So each payroll system works out your tax as if that job were your only income for the whole year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L12",
   "file": "vo/L12.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And when you start a job, you fill out a T D one form. That's where you claim your personal tax credits, starting with the basic personal amount."
  },
  {
   "id": "L13",
   "file": "vo/L13.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "If you fill out both T D one forms the same way, both employers give you that credit. But you only get it once on your tax return."
  },
  {
   "id": "L14",
   "file": "vo/L14.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Hold on to that. It's half of Lena's problem."
  },
  {
   "id": "L15",
   "file": "vo/L15.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, Lena's year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L16",
   "file": "vo/L16.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Job A pays forty-five thousand dollars. Job B pays thirty thousand."
  },
  {
   "id": "L17",
   "file": "vo/L17.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Job A's payroll treats forty-five thousand as her whole income. It withholds about five thousand, two hundred and forty-three dollars over the year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L18",
   "file": "vo/L18.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Job B's payroll treats thirty thousand as her whole income. It withholds about two thousand, four hundred and fifty-two."
  },
  {
   "id": "L19",
   "file": "vo/L19.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Together, about seven thousand, six hundred and ninety-five dollars withheld."
  },
  {
   "id": "L20",
   "file": "vo/L20.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But on her tax return, the C R A adds the two jobs together. Seventy-five thousand dollars of income."
  },
  {
   "id": "L21",
   "file": "vo/L21.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The real income tax on seventy-five thousand: about twelve thousand, seven hundred and five."
  },
  {
   "id": "L22",
   "file": "vo/L22.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Twelve thousand, seven hundred and five, minus seven thousand, six hundred and ninety-five. She owes about five thousand and ten dollars."
  },
  {
   "id": "L23",
   "file": "vo/L23.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's about one hundred and ninety-three dollars that should have come off every paycheque, and didn't."
  },
  {
   "id": "L24",
   "file": "vo/L24.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So where did the gap come from? Two places."
  },
  {
   "id": "L25",
   "file": "vo/L25.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The first one is the basic personal amount."
  },
  {
   "id": "L26",
   "file": "vo/L26.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Both employers gave her the credit. On her return, she only gets it once. So one of those credits was never real."
  },
  {
   "id": "L27",
   "file": "vo/L27.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The second one is quieter. It's the brackets."
  },
  {
   "id": "L28",
   "file": "vo/L28.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Job B's payroll taxed her thirty thousand dollars as if it were the first thirty thousand she earned. At the lowest rates."
  },
  {
   "id": "L29",
   "file": "vo/L29.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But on her return, that thirty thousand sits on top of the forty-five thousand from Job A. In higher brackets."
  },
  {
   "id": "L30",
   "file": "vo/L30.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Neither payroll could see that. Only the tax return can."
  },
  {
   "id": "L31",
   "file": "vo/L31.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The more your two jobs add up to, the bigger this second gap gets."
  },
  {
   "id": "L32",
   "file": "vo/L32.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Here's what this is not."
  },
  {
   "id": "L33",
   "file": "vo/L33.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "It's not that a second job is taxed at a special higher rate. There's no second job tax."
  },
  {
   "id": "L34",
   "file": "vo/L34.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Every dollar is taxed by the same brackets, once your income is added up. The withholding was simply an estimate, and it guessed low."
  },
  {
   "id": "L35",
   "file": "vo/L35.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's the same idea as a refund or a balance owing. We covered that a few videos ago."
  },
  {
   "id": "L36",
   "file": "vo/L36.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, fix number one."
  },
  {
   "id": "L37",
   "file": "vo/L37.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "On the T D one for your second job, there's a box: more than one employer or payer at the same time."
  },
  {
   "id": "L38",
   "file": "vo/L38.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "If your total income will be more than the credits you already claimed on your other T D one, you tick that box and enter zero for the claim amount."
  },
  {
   "id": "L39",
   "file": "vo/L39.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Then that employer doesn't give you the basic personal amount a second time."
  },
  {
   "id": "L40",
   "file": "vo/L40.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For Lena, Job B's withholding goes up to about five thousand, four hundred and eleven dollars a year."
  },
  {
   "id": "L41",
   "file": "vo/L41.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Her total withheld becomes about ten thousand, six hundred and fifty-four. The bill in the spring drops from five thousand and ten, to about two thousand and fifty."
  },
  {
   "id": "L42",
   "file": "vo/L42.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "That's fix number one. Same pay. Same tax. Just a more honest estimate."
  },
  {
   "id": "L43",
   "file": "vo/L43.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, fix number two, for the bracket gap."
  },
  {
   "id": "L44",
   "file": "vo/L44.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The same T D one form lets you ask for extra tax to be deducted from every paycheque."
  },
  {
   "id": "L45",
   "file": "vo/L45.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For Lena, about seventy-nine dollars extra per paycheque at Job B would cover the rest."
  },
  {
   "id": "L46",
   "file": "vo/L46.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Then her return in the spring lands close to zero. No surprise bill."
  },
  {
   "id": "L47",
   "file": "vo/L47.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And if she'd rather keep the money during the year, that's her choice. She just needs to set it aside for spring."
  },
  {
   "id": "L48",
   "file": "vo/L48.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Does any of this change how much tax she pays in total? No. It's about twelve thousand, seven hundred and five dollars either way."
  },
  {
   "id": "L49",
   "file": "vo/L49.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The forms only change when she pays it. A little every two weeks, or all at once in the spring."
  },
  {
   "id": "L50",
   "file": "vo/L50.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "And if you owe a large amount year after year, the C R A may ask you to pay in instalments. Fixing your T D one is usually the simpler route."
  },
  {
   "id": "L51",
   "file": "vo/L51.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Now, C P P and E I."
  },
  {
   "id": "L52",
   "file": "vo/L52.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Each employer deducts C P P and E I separately, as if it were your only job."
  },
  {
   "id": "L53",
   "file": "vo/L53.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "For Lena, Job A takes one point six three percent for E I on forty-five thousand. Job B takes it on thirty thousand."
  },
  {
   "id": "L54",
   "file": "vo/L54.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Together, that's one thousand, two hundred and twenty-two dollars and fifty cents of E I."
  },
  {
   "id": "L55",
   "file": "vo/L55.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "But the most an employee pays in twenty twenty-six is one thousand, one hundred and twenty-three dollars and seven cents."
  },
  {
   "id": "L56",
   "file": "vo/L56.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The extra, ninety-nine dollars and forty-three cents, comes back on her tax return."
  },
  {
   "id": "L57",
   "file": "vo/L57.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "She doesn't have to apply for it separately. It's worked out when she files."
  },
  {
   "id": "L58",
   "file": "vo/L58.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "The same rule applies to C P P. If two employers together take more than the yearly maximum, the overpayment comes back when you file."
  },
  {
   "id": "L59",
   "file": "vo/L59.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "Here's your two jobs checklist."
  },
  {
   "id": "L60",
   "file": "vo/L60.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. Claim your personal amounts on one T D one only."
  },
  {
   "id": "L61",
   "file": "vo/L61.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. On the second job's T D one, tick more than one employer, and enter zero."
  },
  {
   "id": "L62",
   "file": "vo/L62.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. Ask for extra tax per paycheque if your jobs add up to a higher bracket."
  },
  {
   "id": "L63",
   "file": "vo/L63.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Four. When you file, check for C P P or E I overpayments."
  },
  {
   "id": "L64",
   "file": "vo/L64.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Five. If you skip the extra tax, set the money aside yourself."
  },
  {
   "id": "L65",
   "file": "vo/L65.mp3",
   "gap": 1.0,
   "text": "So, what did we learn?"
  },
  {
   "id": "L66",
   "file": "vo/L66.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "One. Each employer withholds as if it's your only job."
  },
  {
   "id": "L67",
   "file": "vo/L67.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Two. Claiming the basic personal amount twice creates a bill."
  },
  {
   "id": "L68",
   "file": "vo/L68.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Three. There's no special tax on a second job. The brackets just add up."
  },
  {
   "id": "L69",
   "file": "vo/L69.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Four. Two forms, set up right, can make your spring bill close to zero."
  },
  {
   "id": "L70",
   "file": "vo/L70.mp3",
   "gap": 0.35,
   "text": "Next on this channel: the complete Canadian tax year, from your first paycheque to your tax return. Subscribe, so you don't miss it."
  }
 ],
